Subject: [PATCH] aiter: tuned bf16 GEMM table for Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95B on gfx950/cu256

Base:        aiter d9e5ef7ce08ee7045d583aed768cff41aa9210fe
Applies to:  /sgl-workspace/aiter
Apply:       git -C /sgl-workspace/aiter apply /work/patches/rejected/0001-aiter-tuned-gemm-qwen3_8_2_4t_a95b.patch
             (equivalently: cp /work/analysis/qwen3_8_2_4t_a95b_bf16_tuned_gemm.csv \
                               /sgl-workspace/aiter/aiter/configs/model_configs/)
             The table is read once per shape behind functools.lru_cache and the
             choice is frozen into the CUDA graphs at capture, so the file must
             be in place BEFORE the server starts. Verify with
             python3 /work/analysis/validate_config.py (exits nonzero on any row).
Verdict:     REJECTED on the key metric -- see below. Filed here, not in the
             parent directory, because +0.08% output_throughput is inside the
             1.03% noise floor. The TTFT gain is real but is not what was asked
             for.
Measurement: baseline 1266.119 tok/s measured locally (n=8, noise floor 1.03%;
             1279.949 documented). Patched: 1267.102 tok/s (n=3) = +0.08%, i.e.
             INSIDE the noise floor and NOT a throughput win. Mean TTFT 10967.4
             -> 10642.0 ms = -2.97%, against a TTFT spread of 0.03-0.29%, which
             is real. Mean TPOT 39.87 -> 40.15 ms (+0.70%, inside its 0.84-1.28%
             spread). gsm8k 5-shot strict-match 0.981046 +/- 0.003756 vs the
             0.978014 +/- 0.004039 baseline gate: unchanged within stderr.
             The kernel rows themselves are validated at 1.04-1.30x; the gain
             does not reach output_throughput because this workload
             (ISL 8192 / OSL 1024) is decode-bound. See FINDINGS.md section
             "1. aiter tuned-GEMM table".

Why
---
The container sets SGLANG_USE_AITER=1, so sglang's UnquantizedLinearMethod.apply
routes every bf16 linear in this model through aiter's tuned_gemm dispatch table
(python/sglang/srt/layers/quantization/unquant.py). That table has no entries for
this model's shapes on gfx950/cu256, so get_GEMM_A16W16_config() falls through to
default_config["libtype"] = "torch" for all of them. The model is a hybrid whose
92 layers issue exactly five dense bf16 shapes per forward:

  (M, 4608, 8192) x92   attention QKV / GDN in_proj_qkvz  (both are 4608 at TP=8)
  (M, 8192, 2048) x92   attention o_proj / GDN out_proj
  (M,  512, 8192) x184  router gate + shared-expert gate_up
  (M, 8192,  256) x92   shared-expert down_proj
  (M,   32, 8192) x69   GDN in_proj_ba

That is 529 launches per forward on the untuned default. Measured against the
HBM roofline the MoE GEMMs run at ~8 TB/s (at the ceiling, nothing to get), but
these dense ones ran at ~3.1 TB/s effective, which is where the headroom was.

How the rows were chosen
------------------------
analysis/tune_one.py enumerates every hipBLASLt solution from
hipb_findallsols() plus triton, torch and asm at splitK in {0,1,2,4,8} for one
shape, validates each against F.linear, and times it by summing GPU kernel
durations out of a torch profiler trace. It is crash-resumable because two of
these shapes fault the GPU partway through the solution list. ~2090 candidates
per shape, at M in {64, 8192, 16384} -- the decode batch (concurrency 64) and the
two prefill batch sizes the workload actually produces.

analysis/validate_config.py then re-times tuned-vs-torch *interleaved*, medians
over 5 rounds, through the real tgemm.mm entry point, and only rows that beat
torch by >3% there are kept. That pass rejected four rows the single-shot search
had liked (64/512/8192 1.078x -> 0.976x; 8192/32/8192 1.234x -> 1.017x;
8192/4608/8192 1.053x -> 0.997x; 16384/32/8192 1.129x -> 0.965x) -- the search
had been measuring drift on those. The eight rows below are what survived, at
the speedups in the right-hand column:

  M=64    N=4608 K=8192  asm splitK=2               1.152x   (decode, x92/step)
  M=64    N=8192 K=256   triton                     1.260x   (decode, x92/step)
  M=64    N=32   K=8192  hipblaslt 438549           1.145x   (decode, x69/step)
  M=8192  N=512  K=8192  hipblaslt 439126           1.187x   (prefill, x184)
  M=8192  N=8192 K=256   hipblaslt 440237           1.044x   (prefill, x92)
  M=16384 N=4608 K=8192  hipblaslt 439304           1.301x   (prefill, x92)
  M=16384 N=512  K=8192  hipblaslt 438362           1.184x   (prefill, x184)
  M=16384 N=8192 K=256   hipblaslt 440237           1.062x   (prefill, x92)

Two shapes are deliberately absent. (8192|16384, 8192, 2048) is already in
llama70B_bf16_tuned_gemm.csv, which picks torch there; aiter merges every
model_configs/*bf16_tuned_gemm*.csv into one table and raises on a duplicate
shape key *after rewriting the source files*, so claiming those two rows for a
1.04-1.06x gain would break the launch and damage another model's config.

The file lands in model_configs/ so get_config_file()'s glob picks it up with no
env var and no change to any existing file; nothing outside these eight shapes
on gfx950/cu256 can be affected. Server flags, environment and workload are
untouched.

